[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The translation compiled fine and got every [38;5;10matoi[0m and [38;5;10mfgets[0m detail right, but it silently swallowed the CWE-129 defect it was supposed to reproduce. [38;5;10mbad()[0m checks only [38;5;10mdata >= 0[0m before [38;5;10mbuffer[data] = 1[0m on a 10-element array; the original Rust absorbed all out-of-bounds stores into a 4096-word padding array, reasoning that only [38;5;10mbuffer[0..10][0m is printed. That reasoning fails at [38;5;10m-O0[0m, which is how [38;5;10mc_src/CMakeLists.txt[0m builds (empty [38;5;10mCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE[0m, so a bare [38;5;10mgcc src/main.c[0m).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
From [38;5;10mobjdump[0m, [38;5;10mbuffer[0m sits at [38;5;10m%rbp-0x40[0m and [38;5;10mmain[0m calls [38;5;10mbad[0m directly, so [38;5;10mbuffer[n][0m writes at [38;5;10m%rbp-64+4n[0m and the neighbourhood is fully determined. Indices 16–17 hit [38;5;10mbad[0m's saved [38;5;10m%rbp[0m, 18–19 its return address, and 26–27 [38;5;10mmain[0m's return address. That predicts crashes at exactly [38;5;10m{16,17,18,19,26,27}[0m — and a sweep of the real binary crashes on exactly that set. Notably 24–25 hit [38;5;10mmain[0m's [3msaved[23m [38;5;10m%rbp[0m and do [1mnot[22m crash, which is why the set isn't contiguous.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Four mismatch classes, all now fixed:[0m[0m
- **16–19, 26–27, and far-out-of-range indices**: C dies with SIGSEGV (139) and empty stdout; Rust exited 0 with full output.[0m[0m
- **Buffering**: those runs produce [3mno[23m output because glibc fully buffers a piped stdout and [38;5;10mexit()[0m never runs. Rust's [38;5;10mLineWriter[0m would have leaked it, so [38;5;10mCStdout[0m now reproduces glibc's mode choice and discards the buffer on the emulated fault.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
A fifth mismatch surfaced only as a flaky test at n=3500: anchoring the headroom on a Rust local's address over-estimated it, because Rust's frames sit deeper than the C's. It's now anchored on the initial stack pointer from [38;5;10m/proc/self/{maps,stat}[0m plus a constant 304 — measured by bisecting the reference under [38;5;10msetarch -R[0m, where the threshold is exact (1259 survives, 1260 faults).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## What can't be matched[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two regions are non-reproducible in the C [3magainst itself[23m, so I documented rather than faked them. The kernel's [38;5;10march_align_stack[0m randomises the stack top by up to 8 KiB per exec (measured spread 8160 bytes), giving a ~2000-index band where the same binary exits 0 on one run and dies on the next. Above ~2×10⁸ the wild address sometimes lands in a file-backed mapping and the C alternates SIGSEGV/SIGBUS (at INT_MAX: 13 × SIGBUS, 17 × SIGSEGV over 30 runs). Strict byte comparison covers indices 0–48 and everything at or above a computed fault floor; the band is covered by an invariant test that holds for both programs.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two of my early test failures were this same mistake — hard-coded index bounds that only work in one shell. The band's position scales with environment size (464 bytes under [38;5;10menv -i[0m vs 14560 with 10 KB extra env), so the tests now derive their bounds at run time.[0m[0m
